project_info:
Transport - Implementation of the shared public bicycle system in conjunction with CIM VDL.: The Launch of an integrated system of conventional and electric bicycles, with multiple stations in strategic points of the city, contributes to the reduction of the use of individual cars, promoting an active alternative with a low ecological footprint, and enhances urban resilience by offering mobility alternatives in situations of disruption caused by extreme weather events (e.g.: road closures, fuel shortages, environmental restrictions)
The entry into operation of the shared public bicycle system in Viseu constitutes a concrete measure to promote active and decarbonized mobility, reducing dependence on individual motorized transport. The project contributes to:
•	Reducing greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions;
•	Improving urban air quality;
•	Encouraging the adoption of sustainable travel habits by the population


https://www.cimvdl.pt/bora/

Transport - Implementation of free passes for young people up to 18 years old and higher education students up to 23 years old.: Measure that aims to promote the early adoption of public transport and reduce future dependence on individual transport, increasing equity in access to sustainable mobility, particularly in more socioeconomically vulnerable groups and contributing to reducing pressure on road infrastructure and urban pollution.
This measure to support young mobility, by guaranteeing free public transport passes, aims to encourage a change in behavior from an early age, encouraging young people to use public transport. The policy contributes to:
•	Reducing the use of private cars;
•	Reducing emissions associated with the transport sector;
•	Promoting social and territorial equity in access to sustainable transport.

Other: Forecasting Local level climate extremes and physical hazards - Multi-Hazard Platform (MHP): Iskandar Malaysia was selected to receive Technical Assistance (TA) from the Climate Technology Centre and Network (CTCN) under the Adaptation Fund Climate Innovation Accelerator (AFCIA) Programme for the development of a Multi-Hazard Platform (MHP) to forecast local-level climate extremes and physical hazards. The project, which began in Q4 2023 and is scheduled for completion in Q4 2025, aims to support early climate risk mitigation through a decision support system designed with inclusive stakeholder input and grounded in an understanding of localized climate impacts—initially focusing on coastal hazards.
Total Cost of project USD 237,850.

The project comprises four main tasks:
Task 1: Inception phase with a detailed workplan;
Task 2: Functional and technical design of the MHP;
Task 3: Development of the prototype MHP;
Task 4: Capacity building for a people-centred forecasting system.

As of July 2025, the prototype phase has been completed, and the MHP is fully functional, producing flood risk maps, rainfall and flood warnings, and impact reports at the mukim (sub district) level. The next stage involves exploring governance models and sustainable funding mechanisms to transition from prototype to a fully operational platform. The project's outcome includes enhanced understanding among local authorities and stakeholders on the operational value, cost, and impact of a multi-hazard system, with direct hands-on experience gained during implementation.

climate_hazard:
Mass movement: The 21st century has seen a high increase in natural disasters related to torrential rains and floods, causing social, environmental and economic losses. During rainy periods, landslides have caused various losses in all spheres, political, economic and social. This environmental problem affects several regions of the world, especially when it comes to landslides or rocks from a surface. It can occur due to natural phenomena, human actions and/or circumstances of mixed impacts. Natural causes can be large volumes of rainfall, temperature changes, among others. In the case of human actions, one of the main reasons is the removal of protection/plant cover from the soil, which is sometimes found in places with steep angles, consequently generating a certain local fragility due to earth movements. Furthermore, inappropriate use of these areas that are already in a weakened situation is also a notable fact, such as, for example, the carrying out of activities, constructions and occupations for housing, causing environmental problems such as landslides to accelerate. In this way, this degradation process occurs due to the combination of climatic factors and the forced fragility of the local system. In the municipality of JundiaÃ­, landslides cause: 1. the need for resources to shelter families who lost their homes; 2. need for investments in community reconstruction; 
3. need for greater waste management, resulting from debris from/or affected buildings; 
4. loss of property; and
5. restriction of the movement of people. 
Landslides cause very serious damage, and it is extremely important to have measures in place to mitigate their effects. Structural measures include engineering works, such as containment works, implementation of drainage systems and redevelopment of areas. Non-structural measures are those aimed at environmental education. | Urban flooding: Flooding occurs when water from rivers, streams, storm drains leaves the drainage bed due to the lack of transport capacity of one of these systems and occupies areas where the population uses for housing, transport, recreation, commerce, industry, among others. These events can be caused by the natural behavior of rivers or amplified by the effect of man-made changes in urbanization, such as the waterproofing of surfaces and channelization of streams. Urbanization eliminates existing natural detention points, reducing the roughness of the basin. The small channels existing in the natural configuration are replaced by drainage piping, the rivers are straightened and replaced and the surface runoff planes are undersized. All of these human interferences in the basin contribute to an increase in surface runoff. The volume that it flowed slowly into the soil and was retained by plants and began to flow in channels, requiring greater drainage capacity in the channels. With the increase in soil impermeability and the consequent decrease in concentration time, the idealized time it takes for water to move from the hydraulically furthest point to the exultation of the basin, the peaks appear larger and earlier in relation to their original configuration, preventing underground recharge from being the same. In addition to the increase in flow and volumes drained, urbanization promotes a deterioration in water quality, as it is harmed by (untreated) cloacal sewage, sediments, solid waste, oils, pesticides and a whole range of pollutants that, during a flood, drag these materials into water bodies. Thus, in relation to the impacts of urbanization on urban waters, perhaps those of a quantitative nature are those that cause the most damage to the population, in the event of floods. Added to this, we have other impacts also caused by floods, such as:
1. impediment to the free movement of people;
2. impediment of circulation of the transport system;
3. closure of businesses;
4. material losses; and 
5. increased demand for cleaning services.

