project_info:
Nature-Based Solutions - Monte Carmelo Urban Park: Land regularization, nature-based solutions and climate adaptation: The project will be developed in the Monte Carmelo Informal Urban Center, currently in the final phase of REURB, where 109 lots are at geotechnical risk and 6 at risk of flooding, in an area of ​​around 40 thousand m². The permanence of families is unfeasible, requiring safe resettlement and integrated planning to reduce geotechnical and
hydrological risks, promote climate adaptation and qualify the territory with Nature-based Solutions (SbN).
The central objective is to integrate land regularization, social inclusion and environmental requalification, through the creation of a multifunctional urban park. The scope includes four axes:
1. Resettlement of families at risk - execution of the Resettlement and Regularization Action Plan (PARR), with relocation to safe areas and social support.
2. Urban and environmental infrastructure - implementation sanitation, sustainable drainage, paving, lighting and public equipment, ensuring adequate urbanization conditions.
3. Requalification of the unoccupied area as a multifunctional park - stabilization of slopes with revegetation; implementation of stairs and rain gardens; afforestation with native fruit species (urban orchard); community gardens with compost bins and cisterns; recycling bin for special waste; leisure and sports spaces (playground, outdoor gym, skateboarding and walking track) and common areas for cultural and collective activities.
4. Community participation and environmental education - holding workshops, awareness-raising activities and co-management processes to strengthen belonging, co-responsibility and social governance.
Key activities include:
● resettlement of families in safe areas;
● implementation of essential infrastructure (sanitation, drainage, lighting, paving and accessibility);
● recovery environmental impact of the degraded area with revegetation, stabilization of slopes and naturalized drainage;
● structuring of spaces for leisure, sports, culture and environmental education;
● implementation of SbNs (community gardens, permeable areas, native afforestation and sustainable drainage);
● intersectoral management with community participation and integration between municipal departments.
The project is innovative in connecting REUB-S's mandatory works - traditionally limited to basic infrastructure - with solutions based on nature. This approach expands the expected results, adds ecosystem services and proposes a new perspective on urban spaces, capable of inspiring and being replicated in other irregular centers in the municipality and in different Brazilian cities.

Other: river management, water quality, waste management, Invasive alien clearing, indigenous tree planting - Transformative Riverine Management Programme (TRMP) on Mavivane River- Phase 2: The Mavivane Transformative Riverine Management Project was proposed to focus on the clearance of invasive alien plants and solid waste found approximately 15 meters from the Mavivane River banks. The focus area of the project is the Mavivane River which carries great historical and heritage tourism significance as it forms part of the King Shaka Heritage Route of the Execution cliffs located on the floodplains of Mavivane, hence its ecological importance to the Municipality.
The Mavivane river is 6.4 kilometers and flows through an area with different land uses, such as residential (informal & formal), commercial and industrial which joins the Mbozamo River which forms part of the Umvoti catchment. The Mavivane River is dynamic and resilient as it encounters development pressures that result in pollution, alien invasive plant species infestation, aging stormwater, and sewer infrastructure, and climate change. These factors negatively affect the river's ability to provide ecological goods and services, causing streamflow imbalance, flooding, and therefore reducing its water quality and quantity. the project implementation plan was divided into 4 manageable river communities' phased out as follows:
 Phase 1- commencing from Nyoniyamanzi informal settlement to Glenhills;
 Phase 2- commencing from Glenhills to Central Business District;
 Phase 3- commencing from Central Business District to Industrial; and
 Phase 4 - commencing from Industrial to Mbozamo River Confluence. The 4 manageable river communities were delineated based on which Ward No. (i.e. 16 and 19, respectively) the Mavivane River falls on. Additionally, the different phases helped in maximising the efficacy of the invasive alien plant control and solid waste removal. Full details can be found in the detailed attached report.

Other: Community involvement and resilience to climate change - SOBROTHERHOOD - Rebuild the lost sense of community in the neighborhood of Sobreiro by reconnecting with nature through biophilic design: The project pointed to a surgical “architectural intervention, which was coated with biophilic inspiration, to be applied in the social enterprise “Jardins do Sobreiro”, in order to enhance the enjoyment of its public space, as well as local biodiversity through the implementation of nature-based measures.
A The central component of the project focused on Community Involvement and included the organization of an Ideas Competition for children and young people from the Gonçalo Mendes da Maia School Group, which was selected taking into account the proximity of educational establishments to Bairro do Sobreiro, as well as a Mentoring Program for residents of the neighborhood over 55 years old and a Training Program based on principles of biophilic design, aiming to promote a greater intergenerational collaboration and connection with nature.

The Main Objectives of the project: 
•	Increase the resilience of the city of Maia to climate change, through the refunctionalization of public space;
•	Increase local biodiversity through the implementation of nature-based measures;
•	Develop dynamic mechanisms to foster inclusive and sharing experiences in the community, through the co-creation of flexible public infrastructure;

Access more information of the project at https://www.cm-maia.pt/institucional/inovacao-e-desenvolvimento/programas-e-projetos/eit-climate-kic and https://sobrotherhood.pt/

Other: Energy sustainability and carbon-neutral balance - SPARCS - Sustainable Energy Positive & Zero Carbon Communities: The project aimed to demonstrate and validate innovative and replicable solutions with a view to achieving positive, intelligent and integrated energy systems, with a particular focus on active community involvement and participation. The main output stands out as having contributed to the definition of a City Vision for 2050, with the identification of 5 preponderant strategic areas: I) Urban Development; ii) Energy Transition; iii) Mobility; iv) Smart and Sustainable City; v) Integrated and Inclusive Cities. The definition of these strategic areas, the result of a long reflection that had its embryo in an initial exercise that involved some elementary school students from the municipality and culminated in reflections/debates carried out by a wide range of technicians from the municipality and various stakeholders with competence in the defined fields, confirms the already long path towards integral sustainability and digital transition that Maia has been designing.

The project's objectives are: performance, with a view to the integrated evaluation of SPARCS interventions carried out in “LIGHTHOUSE Cities”.
•	Implement Community involvement actions, ensuring inclusive, integrated and sustainable urban development.
•	Replicate the solutions demonstrated in “LIGHTHOUSE Cities” in FELLOW cities”, laying the foundations for their adoption and scalability;
•	Produce a City Vision for 2050, in line with the strategy for energy and climate in the European Union.
•	Actively cooperate with other smart city and community projects already supported in the context of the Horizon 2020 program;
•	Support the development of new services, business models and financing instruments;
•	Actively and efficiently promote the SPARCS project and its results at local and European level;

Access more information about the project https://www.cm-maia.pt/institucional/inovacao-e-desenvolvimento/programas-e-projetos/horizonte-europa

Water management - Mathematical modeling of water supply systems in the municipality of Caldas da Rainha - phase 1: This project “Mathematical Modeling of the Caldas da Rainha Distributor System - Pilot Zone of Foz do Arelho” is the first step towards the subsequent hydraulic and energy optimization of the municipal water distribution network. This optimization will result in:
• Better hydraulic performance, consequently increasing the quality of service to the population;
• Increased resilience of the system;
• Reduced energy costs, particularly in lifting systems;
• Reduced ruptures and breakdowns;
• Reduced water losses in the distribution network.
Thus, in the first phase, based on the mathematical and hydraulic model that was developed, the diagnosis of the subsystem in cause, a Pilot Zone (ZP) with around 44km in length (in a total of around 940km of municipal network), corresponding to the Area of Influence of the Foz do Foz reservoirs. Arelho (High), Nadadouro and Foz do Arelho (Lower). Currently underway is the renovation of additional instrumentation to the Foz do Arelho system. The next phase awaits the launch of the tender and will consist of the hydraulic and energy optimization of the Caldas da Rainha distribution system (with a pilot area referring to Foz do Arelho, for subsequent expansion to other areas of the municipality).

Buildings - Rehabilitation and Maintenance of Tornada I and All-World Water Reservoirs: Given the need to carry out the rehabilitation and maintenance of the Tornada I and All-World reservoirs, as they present various anomalies, particularly with regard to cracks, exposed reinforcement, infiltrations, damaged grilles, interior and exterior painting, among others, which could lead to problems of contamination of the stored water, as well as significant water losses, the aforementioned procedure that is already being implemented.
Gains:
- Reduce losses and increase water efficiency, since there will be fewer leaks which mean greater availability of water for human consumption;
- Optimization of storage capacity, avoiding waste;
- Protection of water quality, since maintenance will reduce the risks of infiltration, contamination and degradation of stored water, reducing additional treatment costs (either chemicals or energy).

Other: Research and Innovation - Center for Climate Change Studies: The need to adapt territories to extreme phenomena resulting from climate change requires permanent and effective monitoring of all environmental and anthropogenic variables that influence natural systems, in order to define efficient action strategies. In this sense, resulting from the Municipal Strategy for Adaptation to Climate Change of the Municipality of Figueira da Foz, in conjunction with the installation of a University Center of Coimbra in the Municipality, the opportunity arises to implement the installation of a Center for the Study of Climate Change, as a research center of a scientific and innovative nature, which provides technical solutions that respond to the current challenges of climate change. climatic variables, through monitoring and support tools, with periodic information and weather alerts (sea agitation, strong wind, heavy precipitation, high temperature); periodically the Local Climate Impact Profile. 

This Center will also promote the monitoring and training of municipal technical staff in the face of climate change, which is why it aims to:
•Constitute an internal municipal team to study/plan strategies within the scope of adaptation and mitigation of climate change and consequent risks, involving technicians from all departments of the Municipality in order to create a multidisciplinary, effective and efficient team, capable of boosting the Municipality's climate action;
•Promote the training of municipal technicians and political decision-makers in the field of climate action at the local level, both at the level of adaptation, namely in the assessment of vulnerability to climate change and adaptive management) and at the level of mitigation, in the preparation of inventories of greenhouse gas emissions and implementation of concrete actions that reduce CO2 emissions for the atmosphere;
•Hold a biannual Workshop with members from different departments of the Municipality, in order to create and develop a common understanding on the importance of adaptation and mitigation to climate change and promote the integration of adaptation/mitigation in planning and policies among municipal technicians from different departments of the Municipality.

It is noteworthy that the Municipality of Figueira da Foz already owns a building destined for this pretension, located at the top of Serra da Boa Viagem, and an intervention is planned in terms of requalification works, for the purpose of converting this building into the Center for Climate Change Studies.

Other: Urban Climate Resilience (adaptation of public space, nature-based solutions, awareness raising and education, Preservation of natural resources, risk mitigation, etc.) - UrbanAdaPT Project - Urban Network for Climate Resilience: Within the scope of the Notice (ITI) “Urban Networks” of the Regional Programs NORTE2030, CENTRO2030, LISBOA2030, ALENTEJO2030 and ALGARVE2030 - Pre-qualification, the municipality of Loulé is part of the consortium of the UrbanAdaPT Project - Urban Network for Climate Resilience (led by the Landscape Laboratory, and involving the Municipality of Águeda, the Municipality of Torres Vedras, the Municipal Water and Sanitation Services of Torres Vedras, the Municipality of Mértola. Developed in three phases, this application aims to coordinate and articulate between partners a set of immaterial and material actions that allow the development of an integrated response to climate challenges, promoting resilience in urban environments, in order to enhance efficient and efficient management sustainable development of the territories involved.
The general objectives of the UrbanAdaPT Urban Network are: 
i) Integrated development in urban areas; 
ii) Protection and enhancement of natural heritage;
iii) Mitigation and adaptation to climate change; 
iv) Public awareness and participation in the sustainable development of territories.
These are specific objectives, transversal to all partners that make up the UrbanAdaPT Urban Network:
i) Develop a participatory local governance model that capitalizes on the best practices, technologies and policies for integrated territorial development;
ii) Reduce the effect of heat islands in urban environments;
iii) Promote water efficiency and sustainable water management, improving the urban water cycle water;
iv) Renaturalize and rehabilitate the green and blue corridors and mitigate the effects of floods;
v) Educate and raise awareness, across all ages, on the proposed themes;
vi) Contribute to influence the orientation of municipalities in the transition to a circular economy, promoting the creation of synergies, environmental training, resilience and proactivity of partners in the face of current challenges.
With regard to Integrated Cooperation, this project will: 1. Enhance exchanges between partners; 2. Promote mutual learning and sharing of good practices; 3. Strategically articulate activities, connecting local and interurban dimensions and 4. Establish a replicable model with effective impact at national level.
The application was approved (with maximum marks) in Phase 1 - Pre-qualification Phase, and the consortium is currently awaiting the results of Phase 2 - Action Plan (Invitation Notice MPR-ITI_R_URB-2025-1). After approval of the Action Plan, each entity will advance to Phase 3 - Implementation, with specific notices opening for each project.
The consortium's candidacy proposal includes projects related to thermal mitigation and mitigation of the effects of the urban heat island, adaptation of public space to extreme temperature events and intense precipitation, water efficiency, promotion of water circularity in urban areas and water reuse, rehabilitation of water lines and training, education and awareness projects, in response to which, the sharing of work methodologies, knowledge and good practices can undoubtedly enhance the results to be obtained.
With specific reference to the candidacy proposal of the municipality of Loulé, it includes the following operations/projects:
• LO2 - Study 'Vulnerability to the Urban Heat Island Phenomenon'
• LO3 - Technical Training - Climate Adaptation of Urban Space
• LO1 and LO5 - Circular Schools (three schools)
• LO6 - Climate Adaptation of Urban Space - Water Circularity and Urban Heat Islands
• LO7 - Climate Adaptation of Urban Space - Extreme Precipitation Events
• LO8 - Creation of Shadow Islands in the Middle Urban
• LO4 and LO9 - Miyawaki Forests (three schools - maintenance three schools - design planting maintenance)

Landscape and Jurisdictional Approaches - Manage urban afforestation in the municipality in an integrated manner, thus reducing the emission of greenhouse gases.
: Identify and locate trees on public roads in the municipality and their phytosanitary conditions;
• Identify the variability of existing species;
• Verify the adaptability of species planted on public roads in the municipality;
• Identify trees at risk;
• Identify locations for the implementation of new trees;
• Predict and carry out the adequate maintenance of the municipality's afforestation;
• Identify the limitations for implementing afforestation on public roads found in the
city.

Renewable energy - PROPOSAL
LAND RESTORATION FOR INVASIVE PLANTS IN BIODIVERSITY CULTIVATION
IN SANGGAU REGENCY: The goals of controlling invasive plant populations in Biodiversity Parks include:
1. preventing invasive plants from growing within the park;
2. controlling existing invasive plant populations and preventing their spread;
3. reducing the impact of invasive plants on wildlife habitat destruction and reduction, which can lead to population declines;
4. reducing changes in habitat structure, disturbance patterns such as forest fires, and nutrient and water cycles within the ecosystem caused by invasive plants;
5. maintaining biodiversity, as invasive plants can reduce the number of native plant species and even contribute to species extinction.

